        form: '<table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Original → Paraphrase</th></tr><tr><td>Synonyms</td><td>exaggerated → overstated; recovered → returned to previous levels</td></tr><tr><td>Change word class (nominalization)</td><td>populations have recovered → the recovery of urban populations</td></tr><tr><td>Change structure (active/passive, clause order)</td><td>Remote work has made it possible… → It has become possible, thanks to remote work, …</td></tr><tr><td>Generalize / specify</td><td>nurses, cooks, drivers → essential workers</td></tr></table><table><tr><th>Synthesis language</th></tr><tr><td>Both authors agree that… / Similarly,… / Likewise,…</td></tr><tr><td>While X argues that…, Y maintains that… / X, by contrast, …</td></tr><tr><td>X adds / complements this by… / Taken together, these views suggest…</td></tr></table><p class="small">A good paraphrase keeps the meaning (and the level of certainty!) but changes at least words <b>and</b> structure. Never just reorder the original words.</p>',
